., which match the [-180, 180] range and center around -118\u00b0)\n\nI need to pass all three column identifiers to the filtering function: the station ID column, latitude column, and longitude column. The file is UTF-16 encoded with a BOM marker, and decoding the header reveals `Site,Latitude,(deg)`. Looking at the first data row `7ODM,34.1164082700,-117.0931969700`, I can identify three columns: a site identifier, a latitude value, and a longitude value. The \"(deg)\" notation likely indicates the latitude is in degrees, though the full header structure needs clarification since there's a third numeric column that corresponds to longitude values like -117.09, -117.44, -116.42. The data rows consistently show site codes (7ODM, ACSB, ACSX, AGMT, AGNS, AL2H, ALBH, ALPP, ALTH, ANA1, ANAT, AR27, AR53, ARLI, ARLN, ARM1, ARM2, ASHL) followed by two decimal coordinates. The header appears to be UTF-16 LE encoded with a BOM marker. Decoding it shows `Site,Latitude,(deg)` followed by what should be a longitude column header, though it seems incomplete or oddly formatted. Looking at the actual data values confirms the structure: the first column contains station identifiers, the second has latitude coordinates ranging from the low 30s to mid-40s, and the third column clearly contains longitude values (all negative, ranging from about -117 to -124), which is consistent with California locations.\n\nFor the function call, I need to identify the correct column names. The latitude column is straightforward as \"Latitude,\" but the longitude column header appears truncated or malformed in the output. I should try reading the file with proper UTF-16 decoding to see the complete header, or make an educated guess based on common naming conventions\u2014likely \"Longitude\" or something similar with the \"(deg)\" suffix. has standard column names like Site, Latitude, and Longitude. I'll attempt the filter using common naming conventions\u2014\"Latitude\" for latitude values and likely \"Longitude\" or a variant for the negative values in the third column. The UTF-16 encoding is causing the garbled display, but the underlying structure appears straightforward once I account for that.\n\nLooking at the actual data values rather than the header formatting, I can infer the column purposes: the first column contains site identifiers, the second contains latitude values, and the third contains what should be longitude data. Even though the header display is corrupted, the numeric patterns in the data itself make the column assignments clear. to UTF-8 first, which would make it much easier to read the actual column names. That way I can see the exact header structure without the encoding noise.\n\nThe core issue is identifying the longitude column name. I can see \"Latitude\" clearly in the data, and there's definitely a third column with longitude values around -117 to -118 degrees. The header shows \"(deg)\" somewhere, but I need to determine if that's part of the column name or just a unit indicator. Converting the file encoding would let me read the headers directly and confirm the exact names before setting up the filter. try calling geo_filter with the column names I can identify: \"Site\" for id_column and \"Latitude\" for lat_column. The third column's name is unclear, so I'll use Python to read the CSV header properly with UTF-16 encoding and see the full column names before proceeding. ('Header:', header)\n    for i, row in enumerate(reader):\n        if i < 3:\n            print('Row', i, ':', row)\n        else:\n            break\n\"\n```\n\nThis gives me a clear view of the structure. But I need to reconsider my approach\u2014the tool's auto-detection for latitude and longitude columns won't work reliably here. The documentation warns that scientific datasets often have mislabeled or inconsistent headers, so I can't trust the tool to guess correctly. I need to inspect the actual data values to confirm which columns contain valid latitude ([-90,90]) and longitude ([-180,180]) ranges near my target location, then explicitly pass those column names. Let me read the CSV header and first few rows to identify the correct columns before calling the filter function. The UTF-16 encoding is making it difficult to parse the header clearly from the shell output. I should use Python to read the CSV file directly and see the actual column names. Looking at what I can make out, I see \"Site\" and \"Latitude\" clearly, and the third column contains longitude values. The header appears to show \"(deg)\" but I'm not certain if that's the complete column name or if there's more text. Rather than guessing, I'll write a simple Python script to extract and display the header row so I can see exactly what columns are present. I could try simpler approaches like `file` or `strings` commands, but those might not reliably parse the UTF-16 encoding. Python is the most straightforward way to read the header directly and confirm the exact column names before proceeding with the geo_filter tool.\n\nI'll use a Python one-liner to open the CSV with UTF-16 encoding, extract the header row, and print a couple of data rows to verify the structure. The tricky part is properly escaping the backslashes and quotes in the shell command - I need to double-escape the path separators and handle the nested quotes carefully.\n\nLet me try a simpler approach instead: just attempt the geo_filter with my best guesses for the column names and see what error I get. Based on typical naming conventions, I'm expecting `id_column` as \"Site\", `lat_column` as \"Latitude\", and `lon_column` as \"Longitude\". If any of these are wrong, the tool will report which column it can't find, which will help me diagnose the actual structure.\n\nLooking more carefully at the data itself, the first row shows three comma-separated values: `7ODM,34.1164082700,-117.0931969700`. This confirms there are exactly three columns. The header appears to be \"Site\", \"Latitude\", and something starting with \"(deg)\" - likely the longitude column name is truncated or formatted oddly in the head output. cleaned and the longitude column was renamed to \"(deg)\" or something went wrong with the headers. I need to read the CSV properly to see what's actually there. Let me use Python to check the first few rows and understand the structure:\n\n```bash\npython << 'EOF'\nimport csv\nfilepath = r'D:\\Libraries\\Documents\\projects\\ndp-demo-workspace\\earthscope_stations_clean.csv'\nwith open(filepath, encoding='utf-16') as f:\n    reader = csv.reader(f)\n    header = next(reader)\n    print(\"Header:\", header)\n    for i in range(3):\n        try:\n            row = next(reader)\n            print(f\"Row {i}:\", row)\n        except StopIteration:\n            break\nEOF\n```\n\nThis will show me the actual column names and data so I can figure out which column contains the longitude values and what it's actually called. The UTF-16 encoding makes it difficult to parse the header from the raw output. I can see there are three columns\u2014a station ID, latitude values, and longitude values\u2014but I need to read the file properly with Python to confirm the exact column names, particularly for the longitude field which has an unclear header."}}

